1
00:00:00,543 --> 00:00:02,682
Sync and corrections by n17t01
www.addic7ed.com

2
00:00:02,793 --> 00:00:04,164
(Crows caw)

3
00:00:04,413 --> 00:00:06,315
<i>(Man) It's Movieland's</i>
<i>biggest night--</i>

4
00:00:06,441 --> 00:00:09,093
<i>a night filled</i>
<i>with unimaginable surprises.</i>

5
00:00:09,236 --> 00:00:12,409
<i>Some will go home winners,</i>
<i>and some will go home losers.</i>

6
00:00:12,530 --> 00:00:13,939
<i>But one thing's for sure.</i>

7
00:00:14,062 --> 00:00:16,380
<i>It's the glitziest,</i>
<i>most glamorous evening</i>

8
00:00:16,477 --> 00:00:17,694
<i>of the year.</i>

9
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:22,720
Aw. Moths ate my Oscar carpet.

10
00:00:22,848 --> 00:00:23,852
(Turns on TV) Oh, no.

11
00:00:23,947 --> 00:00:25,462
Where will Brad Pitt walk?

12
00:00:25,569 --> 00:00:27,701
Come on, Mike.
It's exciting.

13
00:00:27,816 --> 00:00:30,371
The Oscars. I like it.

14
00:00:30,481 --> 00:00:31,920
It's a nice bit of escapism.

15
00:00:32,014 --> 00:00:33,724
Yeah, I'm all on board
with escaping.

16
00:00:33,828 --> 00:00:35,811
Well, you're not allowed to.
You're watching it.

17
00:00:35,922 --> 00:00:37,888
It's like the Super Bowl
of Hollywood.

18
00:00:38,025 --> 00:00:39,326
What do you know
about Hollywood?

19
00:00:39,431 --> 00:00:41,204
Hey, I know plenty
about Hollywood.

20
00:00:41,294 --> 00:00:43,656
I do the "People"
crossword puzzle every week.

21
00:00:43,781 --> 00:00:45,561
(Door opens)
And 5 across is shut up.

22
00:00:45,726 --> 00:00:48,535
Well, just five more days,
and I'm off to the Windy City.

23
00:00:48,678 --> 00:00:49,918
What are you talking about?

24
00:00:50,047 --> 00:00:51,099
Oh, the Chicago trip.

25
00:00:51,217 --> 00:00:52,706
Every year,
the fifth grade class

26
00:00:52,805 --> 00:00:54,126
gets to take a trip,
and this year

27
00:00:54,176 --> 00:00:55,748
we're going to Chicago
for three days.

28
00:00:55,855 --> 00:00:57,938
They've been having meetings
about it all year.

29
00:00:58,106 --> 00:01:01,253
You... know
I'm in fifth grade, right?

30
00:01:01,927 --> 00:01:04,720
We get to stay in a hotel,
go to museums,

31
00:01:04,845 --> 00:01:06,746
have meals <i>in</i> restaurants.

32
00:01:06,843 --> 00:01:10,479
- Wow. Exciting. That sounds like
quite a trip. - I know, right?

33
00:01:10,597 --> 00:01:11,837
I'm finally gonna see the place

34
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:14,014
"The Tastes Of Chicago" catalog
is from.

35
00:01:14,791 --> 00:01:16,454
<i>(Sports game playing on TV)</i>

36
00:01:17,543 --> 00:01:18,987
Yeah. He's not goin'.

37
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:21,508
- What?
- Are you kidding me?

38
00:01:21,627 --> 00:01:24,806
This is Brick we're talking
about. Brick alone in Chicago?

39
00:01:24,919 --> 00:01:27,008
He won't be alone.
He'll be with 30 other kids.

40
00:01:27,122 --> 00:01:30,187
Yeah, for about five seconds
until he wanders onto a bus

41
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:33,818
or an El train or a boat
or a beam 30 stories

42
00:01:33,926 --> 00:01:36,417
over the city.
Have you not seen "Mr. Magoo"?

43
00:01:37,747 --> 00:01:39,263
(Indistinct conversations)

44
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:41,419
- Hi.
- Hi.

45
00:01:41,532 --> 00:01:43,873
(Gasps) Oh, my God.
Did you see how weird that was?

46
00:01:44,017 --> 00:01:46,785
That was so weird.
Wasn't it weird?

47
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:48,201
So weird.

48
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:50,097
Okay, I have to tell you
something,

49
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:52,035
but you have to promise
you won't tell anyone.

50
00:01:52,387 --> 00:01:54,122
Did I tell anyone
when you swallowed a fly?

51
00:01:54,223 --> 00:01:56,625
Okay. Well, when Darrin took me
to the Valentine's Day dance,

52
00:01:56,734 --> 00:01:59,640
and we were cleaning up,
our eyes met over the trash can,

53
00:01:59,801 --> 00:02:01,950
- and he kissed me.
- Aah!

54
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:04,469
Oh, my God! So are--
are you guys like a thing now?

55
00:02:04,607 --> 00:02:06,435
I don't know. We didn't
really get a chance to talk.

56
00:02:06,558 --> 00:02:07,747
Maybe it was just
the mirror ball.

57
00:02:07,856 --> 00:02:10,795
Whenever there are mirror balls,
people do <i>crazy</i> things.

58
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:12,200
(Gasps) But what if
he really likes me

59
00:02:12,316 --> 00:02:13,721
but he doesn't know
that I like him,

60
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:16,356
- and that's why he's being so weird?
- That's totally it.

61
00:02:16,473 --> 00:02:17,599
But what if he doesn't
really like me,

62
00:02:17,691 --> 00:02:18,993
and he just got caught up
in the whole romance

63
00:02:19,099 --> 00:02:20,671
of Valentine's Day
and doesn't really know how

64
00:02:20,791 --> 00:02:21,996
to tell me he doesn't like me?

65
00:02:22,107 --> 00:02:23,607
- Well, that makes sense, too.
- Well, which one is it?

66
00:02:23,721 --> 00:02:24,824
- Which one do you think it is?
- What do you think?

67
00:02:24,935 --> 00:02:27,335
- What do <i>you</i> think?
- Oh, my God. I think I really like him.

68
00:02:27,479 --> 00:02:29,030
Unless he doesn't like me back.

69
00:02:29,463 --> 00:02:32,283
Thanks, Carly. You always
give the best advice!

70
00:02:35,103 --> 00:02:36,956
<i>(Frankie) Okay,</i>
<i>so I was pretty darn nervous</i>

71
00:02:37,072 --> 00:02:38,541
<i>about Brick going to Chicago,</i>

72
00:02:38,678 --> 00:02:40,678
<i>but I figured once I heard</i>
<i>the details of the trip,</i>

73
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:42,003
<i>I would feel better.</i>

74
00:02:42,187 --> 00:02:45,085
<i>I was wrong--
very, very wrong.</i>

75
00:02:45,363 --> 00:02:47,098
Now as far as the hotel goes,

76
00:02:47,206 --> 00:02:48,777
we won't have an adult
in each room,

77
00:02:48,904 --> 00:02:51,890
but the kids will be trusted
to monitor themselves.

78
00:02:52,011 --> 00:02:52,809
Oh, God.

79
00:02:52,935 --> 00:02:55,020
They'll have exactly 26 minutes

80
00:02:55,130 --> 00:02:56,961
to wander
around the Observation Deck

81
00:02:57,059 --> 00:02:59,113
on the 103 floor.

82
00:02:59,223 --> 00:03:00,412
No, no, no, no, no,
no, no, no, no, no.

83
00:03:00,521 --> 00:03:02,202
And then it's off
to Gino's East,

84
00:03:02,316 --> 00:03:05,249
where each child will make
their own deep dish pizza

85
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:07,680
in an 800-degree oven.

86
00:03:07,788 --> 00:03:08,972
What are you doing to me?

87
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:12,376
And that brings us to 8:25
and the end of my presentation,

88
00:03:12,484 --> 00:03:16,816
right on time as... always.
Thank you.

89
00:03:17,028 --> 00:03:20,435
Okay, uh, very informative,
Mr. Walker.

90
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:22,967
Now everyone who's interested
in chaperoning,

91
00:03:23,093 --> 00:03:25,975
please put their name
in this bowl.

92
00:03:26,084 --> 00:03:28,117
Remember to fold
your paper squares twice,

93
00:03:28,228 --> 00:03:30,116
once, and once again.

94
00:03:30,457 --> 00:03:31,565
<i>All right, if there was any way</i>

95
00:03:31,671 --> 00:03:33,105
<i>I was letting Brick</i>
<i>go on this trip,</i>

96
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:34,370
<i>I was gonna</i>
<i>have to do something</i>

97
00:03:34,488 --> 00:03:36,902
<i>that goes against everything</i>
<i>I stand for--</i>

98
00:03:37,006 --> 00:03:38,110
<i>volunteer.</i>

99
00:03:38,209 --> 00:03:40,476
Hi, Connie.
Well, listen, you're in luck.

100
00:03:40,593 --> 00:03:41,676
I've cleared my schedule,

101
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:43,636
and I'm available
to chaperone the trip,

102
00:03:43,742 --> 00:03:45,804
so Chaperone Frankie
reporting for duty.

103
00:03:45,922 --> 00:03:48,270
Oh, that's wonderful,
Frankie, but actually,

104
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:50,662
everybody here
wants to chaperone this trip.

105
00:03:50,807 --> 00:03:52,786
This is the crown jewel
of field trips.

106
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:56,041
Oh. Well, I should really be
one of the chaperones,

107
00:03:56,146 --> 00:03:58,655
'cause I haven't volunteered
for anything all year.

108
00:03:58,769 --> 00:04:00,408
Oh, now do you really think
that's fair

109
00:04:00,609 --> 00:04:02,992
for you to get to go
on the most coveted field trip

110
00:04:03,136 --> 00:04:05,201
after not volunteering
for anything all year?

111
00:04:05,304 --> 00:04:07,694
Look, Brick's kind of special,

112
00:04:07,813 --> 00:04:09,933
and not the kind you slap
a bumper sticker on your car

113
00:04:09,979 --> 00:04:11,911
to brag about, so...

114
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:14,616
Sorry. We have
a fair way of doing it.

115
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:16,667
We put all the names
in a big bowl,

116
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:18,818
and we pick four,
and those are the ones

117
00:04:18,950 --> 00:04:20,150
that get to be the chaperones.

118
00:04:20,279 --> 00:04:21,316
Good luck.

119
00:04:21,708 --> 00:04:22,695
(Chuckles)

120
00:04:26,321 --> 00:04:27,281
You know, if I were you,

121
00:04:27,362 --> 00:04:28,979
I wouldn't even wanna go
on this trip.

122
00:04:29,158 --> 00:04:32,283
Kids throw up on buses,
and when one goes, they all go.

123
00:04:32,403 --> 00:04:36,530
Okay, here we go.
Fingers crossed, everyone.

124
00:04:37,171 --> 00:04:38,310
(Papers rustle)

125
00:04:39,905 --> 00:04:40,992
"Jenna Bieler."

126
00:04:41,087 --> 00:04:43,033
(Screaming and laughing)

127
00:04:43,609 --> 00:04:45,206
(Continues laughing)

128
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:47,038
(Papers rustle)

129
00:04:48,075 --> 00:04:49,404
"Connie McDonald."

130
00:04:49,504 --> 00:04:50,953
Oh, that's me. Oh.
(Laughs)

131
00:04:52,484 --> 00:04:54,032
(Papers rustle)

132
00:04:55,335 --> 00:04:56,742
"Julia Laumann."

133
00:04:56,845 --> 00:04:58,370
(Chair scrapes floor) Whoo!

134
00:04:58,494 --> 00:05:00,030
(Laughs)

135
00:05:00,561 --> 00:05:01,728
(Screams and laughter)

136
00:05:02,014 --> 00:05:04,361
And finally...
(Papers rustle)

137
00:05:07,178 --> 00:05:08,580
"Scott Ridgeway."

138
00:05:08,717 --> 00:05:09,625
(Scattered applause)

139
00:05:09,732 --> 00:05:11,519
(Scoffs) W-wait a minute.
Hold on a second.

140
00:05:11,659 --> 00:05:12,835
A man's name?

141
00:05:12,984 --> 00:05:14,436
How is there a man's name
in there?

142
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:16,127
I don't see any men here.

143
00:05:16,244 --> 00:05:17,687
He had a previous engagement.

144
00:05:17,810 --> 00:05:20,242
I'm sorry. Something doesn't
smell right here.

145
00:05:20,367 --> 00:05:22,491
I mean, come on. You just
happened to pick your own name?

146
00:05:22,601 --> 00:05:25,665
We're supposed to believe
that was <i>just</i> a coincidence?

147
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:27,340
This thing is obviously rigged.

148
00:05:27,454 --> 00:05:30,712
I mean, I didn't get picked,
and I put my name in four times.

149
00:05:31,284 --> 00:05:32,862
(Women murmuring)

150
00:05:35,081 --> 00:05:36,840
(Indistinct conversations)

151
00:05:47,134 --> 00:05:48,669
(Coughs loudly)

152
00:05:48,770 --> 00:05:52,778
- Hey, Sue.
- Oh. (Laughs) Hey, Darrin.

153
00:05:52,938 --> 00:05:55,543
Didn't see you there.
So--

154
00:05:55,654 --> 00:05:56,487
Oh, my God!

155
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:58,080
What are you doing
in the senior hall?

156
00:05:58,216 --> 00:05:59,858
No suck-mores allowed
in the senior hall,

157
00:06:00,001 --> 00:06:01,836
unless you're hot,
which you're not.

158
00:06:01,938 --> 00:06:04,983
I'm Axl Heck, and I approved
this message. (Blows raspberry)

159
00:06:05,101 --> 00:06:06,213
Go!

160
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:10,537
Sorry about that, Darrin.
(Chuckles)

161
00:06:16,734 --> 00:06:17,882
Hey, Brick.

162
00:06:18,322 --> 00:06:20,149
I've got a big surprise.

163
00:06:20,390 --> 00:06:22,891
This Saturday, I am taking <i>you</i>

164
00:06:23,009 --> 00:06:25,396
to the book fair
in Terre Haute.

165
00:06:25,722 --> 00:06:27,659
But I'm gonna be in Chicago
on Saturday.

166
00:06:27,773 --> 00:06:31,778
Oh, that's <i>this</i> weekend.
Hmm. (Sighs)

167
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:34,751
What should we do
about that? (Crunches)

168
00:06:34,865 --> 00:06:38,681
Once-in-a-lifetime book fair
or stinky old Chicago?

169
00:06:38,781 --> 00:06:40,502
Well, despite
the poor air quality

170
00:06:40,609 --> 00:06:41,918
and aging infrastructure,

171
00:06:42,170 --> 00:06:43,970
I think it'd still
rather go to Chicago.

172
00:06:44,073 --> 00:06:45,990
Did I tell you about
all the candy I'm gonna buy you?

173
00:06:46,099 --> 00:06:48,714
Oh, in Chicago, we're going
to an old-time candy store.

174
00:06:48,828 --> 00:06:51,327
You know, there's a lot
of bad things about Chicago.

175
00:06:51,488 --> 00:06:53,966
It's cold. There's crime.
(Door opens)

176
00:06:54,108 --> 00:06:55,188
The Cubs.

177
00:06:55,296 --> 00:06:56,704
Hey, Brickster.
(Door closes)

178
00:06:56,824 --> 00:06:58,237
You pumped
for that Chicago trip?

179
00:06:58,383 --> 00:07:00,544
Dottie's super stoked.
Gonna be the trip of a lifetime.

180
00:07:00,632 --> 00:07:02,212
Uh, I don't think it is, Sean.
(Chair scrapes floor)

181
00:07:02,325 --> 00:07:04,151
There's a lot of other
fun trips coming up.

182
00:07:04,328 --> 00:07:05,031
There's a trip

183
00:07:05,124 --> 00:07:06,609
to the waste recycling plant
in April,

184
00:07:06,728 --> 00:07:09,137
so... yeah.

185
00:07:09,927 --> 00:07:11,744
Why don't you go practice?

186
00:07:13,734 --> 00:07:16,037
(Sighs) Look, Brick,
here's the thing.

187
00:07:16,155 --> 00:07:17,659
If I can't be a chaperone,

188
00:07:17,789 --> 00:07:20,016
I just don't feel comfortable
letting you go.

189
00:07:20,147 --> 00:07:22,614
Look, mom,
I understand you're nervous.

190
00:07:22,775 --> 00:07:24,089
But I read books,

191
00:07:24,191 --> 00:07:25,910
and in every one,
the parents are always nervous

192
00:07:26,014 --> 00:07:28,952
about the hero before he goes
off on his great adventure.

193
00:07:29,069 --> 00:07:31,290
But he always goes,
and he's scared,

194
00:07:31,435 --> 00:07:34,551
but he faces the challenge,
and ultimately triumphs.

195
00:07:34,656 --> 00:07:38,159
Well, I watch TV,
and in all the crime shows,

196
00:07:38,277 --> 00:07:40,164
- little kids die.
- Fine.

197
00:07:40,376 --> 00:07:42,621
I guess I'll just be that kid
who didn't get to go

198
00:07:42,796 --> 00:07:44,892
because his mom
was too worried about him.

199
00:07:45,067 --> 00:07:46,212
That'll be fun.

200
00:07:46,344 --> 00:07:48,763
(Sighs) All right, fine.
You can go.

201
00:07:48,879 --> 00:07:51,185
Yes! I'm finally gonna be
like everyone else.

202
00:07:51,312 --> 00:07:52,663
(Whispers) Everyone else.

203
00:07:52,785 --> 00:07:54,008
Whoop!

204
00:07:56,735 --> 00:07:58,582
♪ You run today ♪

205
00:07:58,937 --> 00:08:00,565
♪

206
00:08:03,012 --> 00:08:04,944
(Song ends)

207
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:06,366
Ha ha!

208
00:08:06,472 --> 00:08:08,705
- Oh! Jeez. What the...
- Sorry.

209
00:08:08,833 --> 00:08:11,120
I forgot you guys
were rehearsing today.

210
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:14,116
- Don't mind me.
- What are you wearing?

211
00:08:14,239 --> 00:08:15,439
Why are you dressed like that?

212
00:08:15,563 --> 00:08:18,115
Oh! I don't know.
(Chuckles)

213
00:08:18,477 --> 00:08:20,425
Ah! I was just
gonna get the rake.

214
00:08:20,628 --> 00:08:22,045
I was just gonna do some...

215
00:08:22,521 --> 00:08:23,622
raking.

216
00:08:23,730 --> 00:08:26,311
Oh! (Chuckles)
There's the turpentine.

217
00:08:26,399 --> 00:08:27,522
(Laughs)

218
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:30,003
(Grunting)

219
00:08:31,533 --> 00:08:33,318
Oh! These high heels
sure do come in handy.

220
00:08:33,914 --> 00:08:37,393
Well... you guys can
keep playing. I'll just watch.

221
00:08:37,491 --> 00:08:39,094
Ew! No.

222
00:08:39,221 --> 00:08:40,826
Why not? I mean...

223
00:08:40,930 --> 00:08:42,890
we don't get a lot of practice
in front of people.

224
00:08:42,963 --> 00:08:45,301
Sue is not people!
Now get out!

225
00:08:45,658 --> 00:08:47,528
But, Axl, I--
(Plays chord loudly)

226
00:08:48,417 --> 00:08:49,224
Axl, you--

227
00:08:49,323 --> 00:08:52,473
(Plays chord loudly)
Why are you being so-- aah!

228
00:08:53,183 --> 00:08:55,299
(Strumming loudly)

229
00:09:00,852 --> 00:09:02,990
<i>So Brick was on his way</i>
<i>to Chicago,</i>

230
00:09:03,197 --> 00:09:05,267
<i>and I had made my peace</i>
<i>with it.</i>

231
00:09:05,410 --> 00:09:09,579
Okay. So you don't have
any books on you? None at all?

232
00:09:09,730 --> 00:09:10,698
No.

233
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,002
Eyes on your surroundings
at all times.

234
00:09:13,105 --> 00:09:15,074
No reading, not even street signs.

235
00:09:15,207 --> 00:09:16,768
And I really shouldn't
have to say this,

236
00:09:16,878 --> 00:09:18,779
but please don't put
anything in your mouth

237
00:09:18,920 --> 00:09:21,084
that isn't food.
No exceptions.

238
00:09:21,184 --> 00:09:22,717
Mom, I'll be fine.

239
00:09:23,047 --> 00:09:23,893
(Sighs)

240
00:09:24,957 --> 00:09:26,293
Oh, wait.

241
00:09:43,437 --> 00:09:44,252
Hi.

242
00:09:44,353 --> 00:09:45,313
Frankie Heck.
We met at--

243
00:09:45,378 --> 00:09:46,634
Yeah, I know who you are.

244
00:09:46,748 --> 00:09:48,538
We have all of our chaperones.

245
00:09:48,649 --> 00:09:50,092
Mrs. Heck.

246
00:09:50,311 --> 00:09:53,274
My parent contract
clearly stated

247
00:09:53,393 --> 00:09:55,765
that once you have said good-bye
to your child,

248
00:09:55,894 --> 00:09:59,995
you have 23 seconds
to depart the premises.

249
00:10:00,126 --> 00:10:03,326
Got it. I-I just wanted
to see if he had a buddy yet.

250
00:10:03,446 --> 00:10:05,166
If you could pair him
with someone responsible--

251
00:10:05,290 --> 00:10:06,637
maybe a girl.

252
00:10:07,001 --> 00:10:08,111
We're good here.

253
00:10:08,208 --> 00:10:09,234
(Laughs)

254
00:10:09,327 --> 00:10:10,401
Let's move it out!

255
00:10:10,493 --> 00:10:12,583
Also, I packed
all orange clothes

256
00:10:12,699 --> 00:10:14,526
so he'll be easy to spot.
He wanders.

257
00:10:14,645 --> 00:10:16,191
And if someone could check when
he comes out of the bathroom,

258
00:10:16,294 --> 00:10:18,124
I can't 100% guarantee that he'll zip up.
(Engine starts)

259
00:10:18,217 --> 00:10:20,248
And if those priceless art
like in a museum or something,

260
00:10:20,367 --> 00:10:21,728
be prepared to meet
the guards now,

261
00:10:21,816 --> 00:10:23,387
because he will touch it.

262
00:10:23,493 --> 00:10:24,862
Walk faster.

263
00:10:25,053 --> 00:10:29,895
Okay! Here we go, people.
We're 34 seconds late.

264
00:10:30,422 --> 00:10:31,667
(Door squeaks)

265
00:10:32,190 --> 00:10:33,167
(Sighs)

266
00:10:34,635 --> 00:10:37,775
<i>So just when Sue had decided</i>
<i>it was just one amazing kiss</i>

267
00:10:37,899 --> 00:10:40,562
<i>over a garbage can, and that's</i>
<i>all it would ever be...</i>

268
00:10:40,676 --> 00:10:43,393
♪

269
00:10:44,783 --> 00:10:45,832
Hey.

270
00:10:46,241 --> 00:10:47,669
I wrote a song.
(Music stops)

271
00:10:49,067 --> 00:10:51,726
This song is called
"Betsy-- Not Her Real Name."

272
00:10:51,854 --> 00:10:53,112
(Paper rustles)

273
00:10:53,282 --> 00:10:58,170
(Off-key) ♪ Sometimes it's
easier to sing your feelings ♪

274
00:10:58,278 --> 00:11:02,191
♪ than to say them
to her face ♪

275
00:11:02,464 --> 00:11:05,506
♪ sometimes it's easier
to look in her eyes ♪

276
00:11:05,614 --> 00:11:09,382
♪ when I'm pretending
to tie my shoelace ♪

277
00:11:09,518 --> 00:11:14,457
♪ but the moment our lips met,
I knew I could never dismiss ♪

278
00:11:14,561 --> 00:11:16,716
♪ our magical trash can kiss ♪

279
00:11:16,846 --> 00:11:18,964
♪ it was one moment of bliss ♪

280
00:11:19,051 --> 00:11:21,906
♪ my magical trash can kiss ♪

281
00:11:22,033 --> 00:11:24,002
♪ one moment of bliss ♪

282
00:11:24,181 --> 00:11:26,328
♪ my magical ♪

283
00:11:26,482 --> 00:11:31,711
♪ trash can kiss ♪

284
00:11:32,830 --> 00:11:34,908
Oh, my God.
I wish I was deaf.

285
00:11:34,995 --> 00:11:36,560
That sucked.
That's... (Blows raspberry)

286
00:11:36,948 --> 00:11:39,685
Even Sue thinks it sucked.
Look, she's crying.

287
00:11:39,812 --> 00:11:41,059
(Laughs)

288
00:11:51,288 --> 00:11:53,425
<i>The next morning,</i>
<i>I'd like to say I felt better,</i>

289
00:11:53,550 --> 00:11:56,085
<i>that the little cheery e-mail</i>
<i>update they sent us parents</i>

290
00:11:56,214 --> 00:11:57,405
<i>had eased my concerns.</i>

291
00:11:57,657 --> 00:11:59,513
<i>I'd</i> like <i>to say that.</i>

292
00:12:00,053 --> 00:12:02,908
"Missing boy plus Chicago"?
What are you doin'?

293
00:12:03,035 --> 00:12:04,960
What, you think
"kidnapped plus Chicago"

294
00:12:05,074 --> 00:12:06,372
is a better search?

295
00:12:06,692 --> 00:12:08,115
Trust me.

296
00:12:08,215 --> 00:12:09,782
If anyone's dumb enough
to kidnap Brick,

297
00:12:09,908 --> 00:12:11,688
after a couple days,
they'll bring him back.

298
00:12:11,956 --> 00:12:14,342
I can't believe you're
making jokes about this.

299
00:12:14,462 --> 00:12:16,572
Look, Brick's growing up.
We raised him right.

300
00:12:16,681 --> 00:12:17,800
Hopefully some of it stuck.

301
00:12:17,910 --> 00:12:18,962
Give him a chance
to impress you.

302
00:12:19,062 --> 00:12:21,585
It'll prove you right.
Worrying doesn't do any good.

303
00:12:21,688 --> 00:12:24,413
That's not true. It shows God
that you're paying attention,

304
00:12:24,518 --> 00:12:25,718
that you're not getting cocky.

305
00:12:25,801 --> 00:12:27,328
Who's the person
that always gets the piano

306
00:12:27,461 --> 00:12:28,614
dropped on their head?

307
00:12:28,724 --> 00:12:31,440
The one whistling,
not the one looking up.

308
00:12:31,993 --> 00:12:32,792
How many people you know

309
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:34,784
have gotten a piano
dropped on their head?

310
00:12:35,252 --> 00:12:39,090
"Piano plus head plus death."

311
00:12:39,685 --> 00:12:41,754
Will you cut it out?
Get off the computer.

312
00:12:41,944 --> 00:12:43,119
(Sighs)
I just can't stop thinking

313
00:12:43,230 --> 00:12:44,906
he's in some kind of danger.

314
00:12:45,131 --> 00:12:49,190
Frankie, listen to me.
He was fun while we had him.

315
00:12:49,302 --> 00:12:51,158
Mike, stop doing that.

316
00:12:53,155 --> 00:12:55,893
<i>The next day, I was actually</i>
<i>feeling a little better.</i>

317
00:12:56,010 --> 00:12:59,051
<i>Mike was right.</i>
<i>There was no point in worrying.</i>

318
00:12:59,169 --> 00:13:01,098
<i>I just needed to take my mind</i>
<i>off Brick,</i>

319
00:13:01,209 --> 00:13:04,019
<i>and there's no better</i>
<i>distraction than Oscar night.</i>

320
00:13:04,299 --> 00:13:06,313
<i>And there was so much</i>
<i>to distract me.</i>

321
00:13:06,429 --> 00:13:08,998
<i>There was the pre-pre-show.</i>
<i>The pre-arrival show,</i>

322
00:13:09,136 --> 00:13:11,168
<i>the pre-red carpet,</i>
<i>the arrivals,</i>

323
00:13:11,279 --> 00:13:13,655
<i>the red carpet, the interviews,</i>
<i>the ring cam,</i>

324
00:13:13,778 --> 00:13:15,467
<i>the bling cam,</i>
<i>the post-red carpet,</i>

325
00:13:15,589 --> 00:13:17,587
<i>and the post-interview wrap-up.</i>

326
00:13:18,355 --> 00:13:19,979
<i>(Orchestral fanfare playing)</i>

327
00:13:20,073 --> 00:13:21,715
It's starting!

328
00:13:22,015 --> 00:13:23,624
Aah! Yay!

329
00:13:23,891 --> 00:13:24,970
(Laughs) Yeah!

330
00:13:25,150 --> 00:13:28,163
Okay. Come on.
Everybody fill out a ballot.

331
00:13:28,330 --> 00:13:29,872
I haven't seen
any of these movies.

332
00:13:30,329 --> 00:13:32,524
And unless you're having
an affair, neither have you.

333
00:13:32,641 --> 00:13:33,905
Just fill it out.

334
00:13:34,510 --> 00:13:36,998
- Where are you going? - I'm gonna
call some of the guys from the quarry,

335
00:13:37,132 --> 00:13:38,304
see what their picks are.

336
00:13:38,423 --> 00:13:39,508
Really?

337
00:13:39,621 --> 00:13:41,754
No. I'm going to get a beer.

338
00:13:42,528 --> 00:13:43,869
(Doorbell rings)

339
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:46,284
(Crunches)

340
00:13:48,021 --> 00:13:48,959
(Gasps)

341
00:13:49,836 --> 00:13:50,957
Hi, Darrin.

342
00:13:51,075 --> 00:13:52,072
Hey, Sue.

343
00:13:52,358 --> 00:13:53,512
You look really nice.

344
00:13:53,731 --> 00:13:56,037
Thanks.
It's Oscar night.

345
00:13:57,005 --> 00:14:00,452
Hey. Um... I didn't get a chance
to tell you the other day,

346
00:14:00,605 --> 00:14:04,564
but you are
an amazing singer/songwriter.

347
00:14:04,703 --> 00:14:07,889
Seriously. No, you are
like Jon Bon Jovi.

348
00:14:08,013 --> 00:14:10,362
Hey, what are you doing here?
We practicing tonight?

349
00:14:10,638 --> 00:14:12,073
I just thought I'd drop by.

350
00:14:12,221 --> 00:14:14,399
Oh. Cool.
Let's go to my room.

351
00:14:14,512 --> 00:14:16,738
Or y-you could watch
the Oscars.

352
00:14:16,955 --> 00:14:18,607
(Laughter)

353
00:14:19,260 --> 00:14:21,146
Watching the Oscars
could be fun.

354
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:22,723
Really?

355
00:14:23,164 --> 00:14:24,671
Guess we could goof on 'em.

356
00:14:24,774 --> 00:14:27,819
Hey, Darrin.
Here. Take a ballot.

357
00:14:27,929 --> 00:14:29,058
Thanks.

358
00:14:30,272 --> 00:14:31,832
Why are they called
the Oscars, anyway?

359
00:14:31,942 --> 00:14:33,433
Why not the Normans
or the Felixes?

360
00:14:33,593 --> 00:14:35,973
(Laughs) Or the Darrins.

361
00:14:36,169 --> 00:14:38,658
(Laughs) And why the red carpet?
Why not a blue one?

362
00:14:38,795 --> 00:14:40,223
(Laughs)

363
00:14:40,364 --> 00:14:41,863
That's hilarious.

364
00:14:43,031 --> 00:14:45,715
Uh, here. I'll help you
with your ballot.

365
00:14:45,843 --> 00:14:48,443
If somebody's boob doesn't
pop out soon, I'm outta here.

366
00:14:48,549 --> 00:14:49,269
Seriously, Darrin.

367
00:14:49,362 --> 00:14:50,639
This is boring. Let's go.

368
00:14:50,733 --> 00:14:52,122
Oh, come on. I wanna stay.

369
00:14:52,232 --> 00:14:54,627
I really like my choice
for best sound mixing.

370
00:14:55,367 --> 00:14:56,393
(Dog barking in distance)

371
00:14:57,043 --> 00:14:59,218
<i>(Applause, orchestra playing)</i>

372
00:14:59,292 --> 00:15:02,235
(Chuckles)
Documentary short. Boom.

373
00:15:02,405 --> 00:15:06,251
Aw! I didn't know "Kiko's Bike"
was about Ugandan warlords.

374
00:15:06,370 --> 00:15:09,115
I would never have picked
"Mandel Bread For Nana."

375
00:15:09,236 --> 00:15:11,202
Oh, Darrin!
What are you doin'?

376
00:15:11,583 --> 00:15:13,177
(Both laughing)

377
00:15:15,054 --> 00:15:16,045
What?

378
00:15:16,147 --> 00:15:17,014
Shove over.

379
00:15:17,122 --> 00:15:19,343
- Just sit there.
- That's my lucky seat.

380
00:15:19,477 --> 00:15:21,157
I picked best costume
in that seat.

381
00:15:21,436 --> 00:15:22,844
Are you... (Groans)

382
00:15:22,958 --> 00:15:26,008
Oh! Kate Winslet and Bill Paxton
are about to present.

383
00:15:26,222 --> 00:15:28,708
That's Cate Blanchett
and Bill Pullman.

384
00:15:29,078 --> 00:15:31,372
They really should make them
wear name tags.

385
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:33,641
Maybe you should suggest that

386
00:15:33,752 --> 00:15:35,887
at the next "People" magazine
crossword meeting.

387
00:15:35,998 --> 00:15:37,794
You know you're wrecking this
for me.

388
00:15:39,011 --> 00:15:42,929
Hey, Brick. Brick?

389
00:15:43,176 --> 00:15:46,261
What are doing here?
You're home. What happened?

390
00:15:46,370 --> 00:15:48,010
Well, we got back
to the school,

391
00:15:48,131 --> 00:15:50,218
and everyone was real excited
to see their parents,

392
00:15:50,405 --> 00:15:53,478
and I thought you'd be there,
but you weren't.

393
00:15:53,581 --> 00:15:54,897
All the other parents
were there.

394
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:56,674
A lot of them had
"Welcome Home" signs,

395
00:15:56,786 --> 00:15:58,068
and I was waiting and waiting,

396
00:15:58,177 --> 00:16:00,588
thinking you'd come,
but you didn't.

397
00:16:00,962 --> 00:16:02,897
So I got a ride home
with Mrs. Donahue,

398
00:16:03,016 --> 00:16:05,562
and she couldn't believe that
you wouldn't have picked me up.

399
00:16:05,691 --> 00:16:06,957
She thought something
must have happened to you,

400
00:16:07,075 --> 00:16:08,856
but... I guess not.

401
00:16:08,977 --> 00:16:11,529
<i>And</i> the Oscar
for worst mother goes to--

402
00:16:11,622 --> 00:16:13,382
No, no, no, no.
That's not right.

403
00:16:13,493 --> 00:16:14,891
I was supposed to pick you up
tomorrow.

404
00:16:15,004 --> 00:16:18,353
It was a 3-day trip--
Friday, Saturday, Su--

405
00:16:19,015 --> 00:16:20,630
<i>♪</i>

406
00:16:21,021 --> 00:16:22,672
I can still buy you candy.

407
00:16:22,818 --> 00:16:24,498
You were worried about <i>me</i>
being irresponsible,

408
00:16:24,607 --> 00:16:26,222
and you didn't even pick me up.

409
00:16:26,336 --> 00:16:27,504
All right,
I think you're stating

410
00:16:27,615 --> 00:16:28,958
what we all already know,

411
00:16:29,069 --> 00:16:31,201
but you're home now.
So come on. Sit down.

412
00:16:31,302 --> 00:16:32,889
I wanna hear
all about your trip.

413
00:16:33,122 --> 00:16:35,741
- Ah, forget it.
- No. Come on, Brick.

414
00:16:35,870 --> 00:16:38,937
I'm sorry.
I wanna hear all about it.

415
00:16:39,056 --> 00:16:40,333
Put in a tape.

416
00:16:40,777 --> 00:16:43,166
I'm gonna go watch basketball
in the garage.

417
00:16:43,734 --> 00:16:45,679
<i>♪</i>

418
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:48,994
Brr! I'm cold.

419
00:16:49,714 --> 00:16:51,147
(Singsongy) Boring!

420
00:16:51,256 --> 00:16:53,858
Your speech is boring!
Play him off. Come on.

421
00:16:54,033 --> 00:16:55,984
Oh, if you tell your kids
to go to sleep,

422
00:16:56,109 --> 00:16:58,595
I'm gonna shoot myself.
(Laughs)

423
00:16:58,726 --> 00:17:00,471
<i>(Man) I'd like to thank</i>
<i>the Academy...</i>

424
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:07,618
Aah!

425
00:17:13,147 --> 00:17:15,792
Aah! No!

426
00:17:16,261 --> 00:17:17,325
No way!

427
00:17:17,441 --> 00:17:18,673
Absolutely not!

428
00:17:18,785 --> 00:17:20,545
- Axl, you don't underst--
- Go to your room!

429
00:17:20,585 --> 00:17:22,710
- You can't tell me to--
- Go to your room!

430
00:17:23,304 --> 00:17:24,949
<i>(TV playing indistinctly)</i>

431
00:17:25,085 --> 00:17:27,847
What are you doing?
Are you insane?!

432
00:17:27,959 --> 00:17:29,762
(Stammers) Is this 'cause
you feel sorry for her?

433
00:17:29,878 --> 00:17:31,479
- No. I like her.
- Is this 'cause I peed

434
00:17:31,570 --> 00:17:33,412
in your Mountain Dew bottle
when we were 7?

435
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:34,463
That was you?

436
00:17:34,577 --> 00:17:36,042
Well... you are not allowed.

437
00:17:36,158 --> 00:17:38,346
You are not allowed
to date my sister. I forbid it!

438
00:17:38,455 --> 00:17:39,441
But I really like her.

439
00:17:39,544 --> 00:17:42,181
Well... (Stammers and sighs)
Fine.

440
00:17:42,309 --> 00:17:44,006
I'm gonna date your mom.
Ha ha!

441
00:17:44,111 --> 00:17:45,813
You better get in there,
because she just met a guy

442
00:17:45,937 --> 00:17:47,031
on Christian Mingle.

443
00:17:47,158 --> 00:17:48,736
I'm gonna date your mom,
and then I'm gonna marry her,

444
00:17:48,869 --> 00:17:49,891
and then I'll be your stepdad,

445
00:17:50,022 --> 00:17:52,057
and then I will forbid you
from dating my sister.

446
00:17:52,176 --> 00:17:53,896
- Axl, we--
- Name one thing you see in her!

447
00:17:53,976 --> 00:17:55,309
- One!
- Well, she--

448
00:17:55,452 --> 00:17:57,743
I don't wanna hear it!
Ohh! (Trills lips)

449
00:17:58,139 --> 00:17:58,773
Ohh!

450
00:17:58,886 --> 00:18:02,312
Dad, there are <i>things</i>
going on in this house

451
00:18:02,451 --> 00:18:05,840
that you need to know about
right now, and mom, too.

452
00:18:06,077 --> 00:18:07,955
Bedroom! Set!

453
00:18:13,044 --> 00:18:15,422
Now grab a bag to barf in
before I tell you this

454
00:18:15,548 --> 00:18:18,283
'cause I have got
some disgusting news.

455
00:18:18,391 --> 00:18:20,067
You ready? All right?

456
00:18:20,195 --> 00:18:24,592
Sue and my ex-friend Darrin
think they like each other.

457
00:18:24,727 --> 00:18:26,561
Yeah. Absorb that.

458
00:18:26,938 --> 00:18:28,270
They're gonna do stuff.

459
00:18:28,415 --> 00:18:30,775
(Chuckles) Maybe not yet.
But they're gonna do stuff,

460
00:18:30,904 --> 00:18:33,598
and... (Gags and grunts)
I can't even talk about this.

461
00:18:33,719 --> 00:18:35,113
I'm making myself sick.

462
00:18:35,323 --> 00:18:37,937
Darrin? Really? Huh.

463
00:18:38,116 --> 00:18:39,932
Well, Axl, Sue's getting older,

464
00:18:40,048 --> 00:18:42,008
and you have to expect things
like this to happen.

465
00:18:42,082 --> 00:18:44,740
- They spend a lot of time toge--
- No! <i>No!</i>

466
00:18:44,854 --> 00:18:46,134
Now I know
I've said this before,

467
00:18:46,215 --> 00:18:48,371
but this time I mean it!
Do some parenting!

468
00:18:48,453 --> 00:18:49,734
Make them stop!

469
00:18:49,829 --> 00:18:51,949
Maybe you should worry
a little less about me flushing

470
00:18:51,952 --> 00:18:53,619
and more about who
your daughter's dating.

471
00:18:54,147 --> 00:18:55,192
Hmm?

472
00:18:55,299 --> 00:18:56,703
Brr! (Grunts)

473
00:18:57,064 --> 00:18:58,163
(Door slams)

474
00:18:58,295 --> 00:19:02,109
Well... this is interesting.

475
00:19:02,244 --> 00:19:05,331
Hmm. I guess she was bound
to date one of Axl's friends.

476
00:19:05,458 --> 00:19:07,238
Yeah. I don't like it.

477
00:19:07,419 --> 00:19:10,568
He's too old, he's too dumb,
and he's too Darrin.

478
00:19:10,670 --> 00:19:12,698
Well, yes,
he's all those things,

479
00:19:12,843 --> 00:19:15,976
but he's also very sweet
and harmless.

480
00:19:16,113 --> 00:19:18,394
That's what the neighbors
always say about serial killers.

481
00:19:18,645 --> 00:19:20,638
Mike, we've known his family
for years.

482
00:19:20,756 --> 00:19:22,599
They're good people.
He's a nice kid.

483
00:19:22,709 --> 00:19:24,025
Let's not overreact.

484
00:19:24,131 --> 00:19:25,645
I can't believe
you're not worried about this.

485
00:19:25,753 --> 00:19:27,323
He's 18, and he looks 30.

486
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:29,946
Well, you're the one who said
they're getting older.

487
00:19:30,067 --> 00:19:31,384
They're growing up.

488
00:19:31,498 --> 00:19:33,727
We have to trust
that we've done our job

489
00:19:33,848 --> 00:19:36,154
and that Sue's ready
to make good decisions.

490
00:19:36,291 --> 00:19:38,767
You said you don't worry,
so just don't worry.

491
00:19:38,873 --> 00:19:41,182
Nobody likes being given
their own advice, Frankie.

492
00:19:41,386 --> 00:19:42,888
Well, what are we
supposed to do?

493
00:19:42,997 --> 00:19:44,838
We can't tell her
she can't go out with him.

494
00:19:44,965 --> 00:19:47,389
She already had car dates
with Matt.

495
00:19:47,498 --> 00:19:48,494
- She did?
- Mm.

496
00:19:48,605 --> 00:19:50,307
- Where was I?
- You...

497
00:19:50,648 --> 00:19:52,051
I'm gonna talk to this kid.

498
00:19:52,688 --> 00:19:55,958
(Sighs)
Whatever happened to Brad?

499
00:19:56,147 --> 00:19:57,975
I liked it when
she was dating Brad.

500
00:19:59,079 --> 00:20:00,036
(Door closes)

501
00:20:02,083 --> 00:20:03,553
(Sighs)

502
00:20:04,996 --> 00:20:07,329
So I understand you're
interested in taking out Sue.

503
00:20:07,456 --> 00:20:08,692
Yes, sir.

504
00:20:09,911 --> 00:20:12,251
I just wanna remind you
that I work at a quarry,

505
00:20:12,369 --> 00:20:13,997
and I have access to dynamite.

506
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:16,973
Yeah, I know.
You have the coolest job.

507
00:20:17,700 --> 00:20:19,892
I think you're missing
what I'm saying here, Darrin.

508
00:20:20,070 --> 00:20:22,090
I'm saying if you're gonna
go out with Sue,

509
00:20:22,226 --> 00:20:25,213
when you're with <i>her,</i>
I want you to picture my face.

510
00:20:26,303 --> 00:20:27,199
No offense, sir,

511
00:20:27,327 --> 00:20:28,763
but that's sort of
a mood killer.

512
00:20:28,980 --> 00:20:31,095
I really like her, sir,
and I will treat her

513
00:20:31,206 --> 00:20:32,222
with the utmost respect,

514
00:20:32,350 --> 00:20:34,640
and I would never do
anything to hurt her.

515
00:20:36,272 --> 00:20:39,919
Ugh. All right.
I guess, fine.

516
00:20:40,032 --> 00:20:41,127
Thanks, Mr. Heck.

517
00:20:41,224 --> 00:20:43,073
I really appreciate
the vote of confidence.

518
00:20:43,182 --> 00:20:44,389
And I'm gonna take you up
on that offer

519
00:20:44,495 --> 00:20:46,092
to come see the quarry.

520
00:20:49,387 --> 00:20:51,720
<i>So another Oscar night</i>
<i>had come and gone.</i>

521
00:20:51,844 --> 00:20:52,973
<i>There were winners...</i>

522
00:20:53,070 --> 00:20:54,110
We're going out
for fro-yo.

523
00:20:54,123 --> 00:20:55,749
We'll be back
in half an hour. Bye!

524
00:20:56,874 --> 00:20:58,493
<i>There were losers...</i>

525
00:20:59,406 --> 00:21:01,215
<i>and there were surprises...</i>

526
00:21:01,344 --> 00:21:03,824
(Camera beeps) Ooh, and here's a
picture of me at Wrigley Field.

527
00:21:03,829 --> 00:21:06,260
I didn't know you guys
went to Wrigley Field.

528
00:21:06,377 --> 00:21:08,016
Oh, no, just me.

529
00:21:12,109 --> 00:21:15,921
Sync and corrections by n17t01
www.addic7ed.com

